Let S denote the unit circle (regarded as a topological graph) and let T_3 denote the triod (3-od), i.e. the graph consisting of three arcs joined at a single common endpoint. For finite topological graphs X and Y, a continuous embedding f: K → X × Y of a finite graph K is called basic if every continuous function φ: f(K) → ℝ can be written as φ(x,y) = α(x) + β(y) for some continuous functions α: π_X(f(K)) → ℝ and β: π_Y(f(K)) → ℝ, where π_X, π_Y are the coordinate projections. (This is the product-space analogue of a basic embedding into ℝ² = ℝ × ℝ.)

Give a structural (combinatorial) characterization, in terms of forbidden subgraphs or other intrinsic features of K, of those finite graphs K that admit a basic embedding into each of the following product spaces:
(a) ℝ × S;
(b) T_3 × S;
(c) S × S;
(d) X × Y, for arbitrary finite graphs X and Y.

